      "source": [
        "# SEC plot workflow: batch SVG and web GUI\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook documents the two ways this repo produces Arcadia-styled SEC (size-exclusion chromatography) plots:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Batch SVG generation** — Python script reading Excel files from a `data/` folder and writing SVGs to `output/`.\n",
        "2. **Web GUI** — Flask backend plus HTML front end for uploading CSV, editing legend text, and generating SVG.\n",
        "\n",
        "Organization and documentation use relative paths only, clear structure, and no spaces in directory names.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Data:** Place Excel files (`.xlsx`) with a **Data** sheet (same layout as described in the README) in the **`data/`** folder. The batch script reads from `data/` and writes to `output/`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## 1. Batch SVG generation (local script)\n",
        "\n",
        "The script `plot_sec_to_svg.py`:\n",
        "- Reads all `.xlsx` files from a single input directory (here: `data/`).\n",
        "- For each file, loads the **Data** sheet and detects column pairs (Volume, signal), stopping at `Pk_*` columns.\n",
        "- Builds series with peak volumes, applies Arcadia colors and legend order (No Treatment, TCEP, EDTA, TCEP + EDTA), and renders one SVG per file.\n",
        "- Writes SVGs to an output directory (here: `output/`).\n",
        "\n",
        "Below we run the same logic from the notebook using relative paths so the repo is self-contained and path-agnostic."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "from pathlib import Path\n",
        "import sys\n",
        "\n",
        "# Run from repo root so plot_sec_to_svg is importable; data/ and output/ are relative to cwd.\n",
        "REPO_ROOT = Path.cwd()\n",
        "if str(REPO_ROOT) not in sys.path:\n",
        "    sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO_ROOT))\n",
        "\n",
        "DATA_DIR = REPO_ROOT / \"data\"\n",
        "OUTPUT_DIR = REPO_ROOT / \"output\"\n",
        "OUTPUT_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "from plot_sec_to_svg import get_series_pairs, render_sec_svg\n",
        "\n",
        "xlsx_files = sorted(DATA_DIR.glob(\"*.xlsx\"))\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Single-file example:** Load one Excel file from `data/`, get series pairs from the Data sheet, render to SVG, and save to `output/`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "39271e37",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "if not xlsx_files:\n",
        "    print(\"No .xlsx files in data/. Add Excel files (with a 'Data' sheet) to run the batch.\")\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    path = xlsx_files[0]\n",
        "    df = pd.read_excel(path, sheet_name=\"Data\", header=0)\n",
        "    pairs = get_series_pairs(df)\n",
        "    title = path.stem.replace(\"_\", \" \").replace(\" (2)\", \"\").replace(\" (27)\", \"\")\n",
        "    svg = render_sec_svg(pairs, title)\n",
        "    out_path = OUTPUT_DIR / (path.stem + \".svg\")\n",
        "    out_path.write_text(svg, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n",
        "    print(f\"Saved {out_path.name}\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Batch run:** Loop over all `.xlsx` files in `data/` and write one SVG per file (same behavior as running `python plot_sec_to_svg.py` when the script is configured to use `data/` and `output/`)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "for excel_path in xlsx_files:\n",
        "    xl = pd.ExcelFile(excel_path)\n",
        "    if \"Data\" not in xl.sheet_names:\n",
        "        print(f\"  Skip {excel_path.name}: no 'Data' sheet\")\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "    df = pd.read_excel(excel_path, sheet_name=\"Data\", header=0)\n",
        "    pairs = get_series_pairs(df)\n",
        "    if not pairs:\n",
        "        print(f\"  Skip {excel_path.name}: no series found\")\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "    title = excel_path.stem.replace(\"_\", \" \").replace(\" (2)\", \"\").replace(\" (27)\", \"\")\n",
        "    svg = render_sec_svg(pairs, title)\n",
        "    out_path = OUTPUT_DIR / (excel_path.stem + \".svg\")\n",
        "    out_path.write_text(svg, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n",
        "    print(f\"  Saved {out_path.name}\")\n",
        "print(\"Done.\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## 2. Web GUI implementation\n",
        "\n",
        "The web UI lets users upload a CSV (same structure as the Data sheet), adjust plot title and legend text, and download an SVG. It has two parts:\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2.1 Backend (Flask)\n",
        "\n",
        "- **`app.py`** serves the single-page HTML and two API endpoints:\n",
        "  - `POST /api/suggest-labels`: accepts `csv_text` and `series` (column mapping); returns `legend_title` and `labels` as they would appear on the figure (so the user can edit them).\n",
        "  - `POST /api/plot`: accepts `csv_text`, `series`, `title`, and optional `legend_title` and `legend_labels`; returns the SVG string.\n",
        "- Plotting is done by `plot_sec_to_svg.render_sec_svg()` and related helpers; `matplotlib` is set to the `Agg` backend so it runs safely in a request thread (no GUI)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Key imports and routes (from app.py). Do not run Flask inside the notebook in production.\n",
        "# To run the GUI: from the repo root run \"python app.py\", then open http://127.0.0.1:5000\n",
        "\n",
        "import io\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "from plot_sec_to_svg import pairs_from_mapping, render_sec_svg, suggest_legend\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example: build pairs from CSV text and column mapping, then get suggested legend\n",
        "def demo_suggest(csv_text: str, series: list) -> tuple:\n",
        "    df = pd.read_csv(io.StringIO(csv_text), header=0)\n",
        "    pairs = pairs_from_mapping(df, series)\n",
        "    return suggest_legend(pairs) if pairs else (\" - Condition\", [])\n",
        "\n",
        "# series = [{\"x_col\": 0, \"y_col\": 1, \"name\": \"BSA_standards\"}, ...]\n",
        "# legend_title, labels = demo_suggest(csv_text, series)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 2.2 Front end (HTML/JS)\n",
        "\n",
        "- **`sec_plot_gui.html`** is a single page: file input for CSV, table for legend title and legend lines, and a \"Generate SVG\" button.\n",
        "- On file load, the page parses the CSV (e.g. with Papa Parse), auto-detects column pairs (Volume + signal, stopping at `Pk_*`), then calls `/api/suggest-labels` to pre-fill the legend title and legend line fields with the same text that would appear on the figure.\n",
        "- The user edits those fields as needed; on \"Generate SVG\", the page sends `csv_text`, `series`, `title`, `legend_title`, and `legend_labels` to `/api/plot` and displays the returned SVG with a download link.\n",
        "\n",
        "No server-side file storage: all data stays in the browser and in the request/response. The `data/` folder is only for the batch Excel workflow above."
      ]
    }
